  "query_preview": "../Saturday through Thursday/...Upper level features \ncontinue to show a rather active pattern through the long range \nperiod. A broad upper trough stretching from the Russian Far East \nto NW Canada will be the main weather maker for the weekend into \nearly next week. The trough is anchored by an upper low that \neventually positions itself over SW mainland Alaska. The broad \ntrough stays in place into early next week, but several short \nwaves are evident rotating around the southern edge of it. There \nare indications now that the trough will then sharpen and narrow \nover the Gulf of Alaska by mid next week as ridging begins to \nbuild over the aleutians and Bering Sea.\n\nAt the surface, the weekend into early next week is looking \ninteresting with a strong broad area of low pressure moving into \nthe gulf from the SW and heading toward the northern gulf coast. \nThe track this low is taking is shaping up for at least a day and \na half of strong and moist onshore flow from the SW for the entire\npanhandle and gulf. Winds in the gulf will show widespread 25 to \n35 kt winds Sat into Sunday, and the northern inner channels could\nsee several areas of 20 to 30 kt during the same time period. As \nfor rainfall, expected 24 hour rainfall amounts are getting into \nthe 1.5 to 3 inch range for many areas for around 2 to 3 days in a\nrow starting Friday. Heaviest rain rates still appear to be from \nSaturday evening into Sunday. Forecast 24 hour annual return \nintervals from the NBM are showing greater then 50 percent \nprobability of exceeding 2 yr return intervals in many parts of \nthe northern inner channels by Sunday afternoon and greater then \n15 percent for exceeding 5 year return intervals in some \nlocations. \n\nIn addition to the stronger onshore SW flow the system will \nbring, it its wake is a shot of cooler air that will be coming in \nfrom the west. Forecast convective indices are already indicating \nthat Sunday into Monday may be rather convective/showery in nature\nas the cooler air moves in and the potential is there for some \nthunderstorms across a large area of the gulf and outer coast at \nthat time. Retained the area of slight chance and chance thunder \nthat has been in the forecast, though it now looks like that \npotential for thunderstorms could linger into Monday night now.\n\nDamp weather continues into the first half of next week with \nonshore flow continuing, but showers and W to SW winds will not be\nas strong. There are indications of more systems moving into the \ngulf next week, with agreement growing on a low coming up from the\nS Tuesday night into Wednesday. However timing of that system and\nwhere it goes once it gets into the gulf is still an open ended \nquestion at the moment.",
